[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Control Structures and Functions in Go (Golang)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go is designed for simplicity, readability, and efficiency. Unlike other languages that provide multiple looping constructs, Go uses the 'for' loop as its sole looping mechanism. Functions in Go are first-class citizens and support multiple return values, making error handling idiomatic and robust."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Statements: If-Else"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Go, conditional statements do not require parentheses around the condition, but the curly braces are mandatory. Go also allows an optional statement to execute before the condition evaluation."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    score := 85

    if score >= 90 {
        fmt.Println("Grade: A")
    } else if score >= 75 {
        fmt.Println("Grade: B")
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Grade: C")
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Ubiquitous For Loop"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go streamlines repetition by dropping 'while' and 'do-while' loops. The 'for' loop handles all iterative patterns: standard three-component loops, condition-only loops (acting as while loops), and infinite loops."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    // Standard for loop
    for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
        fmt.Printf("Iteration: %d\n", i)
    }

    // While-style loop
    n := 1
    for n < 5 {
        n *= 2
    }
    fmt.Printf("Final n: %d\n", n)
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Switch Statements and Automatic Break"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go switch cases break automatically by default unless explicitly terminated with the 'fallthrough' keyword. This design prevents common bugs found in languages like C or Java."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    day := "Tuesday"

    switch day {
    case "Monday":
        fmt.Println("Start of the work week")
    case "Tuesday", "Wednesday":
        fmt.Println("Midweek hustle")
    default:
        fmt.Println("Weekend is near")
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Functions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions in Go are defined using the 'func' keyword. Types are specified after the variable name. Go functions can return multiple values, a feature frequently utilized for returning error objects alongside results."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
    "errors"
    "fmt"
)

func divide(a, b float64) (float64, error) {
    if b == 0 {
        return 0, errors.New("division by zero")
    }
    return a / b, nil
}

func main() {
    result, err := divide(10, 2)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Error:", err)
    } else {
        fmt.Printf("Result: %.2f\n", result)
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conditional expressions do not need surrounding parentheses, but braces are compulsory."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'for' loop is the only looping construct in Go, serving multiple iterative paradigms."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Switch cases break automatically, eliminating accidental fallthrough bugs."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Functions support multiple return values, facilitating clean and idiomatic error handling."}]
